<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">MethodsX</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">MethodsX</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>MethodsX</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="epub">2215-0161</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Elsevier</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41378376</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12686638</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">S2215-0161(25)00569-2</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.mex.2025.103725</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">103725</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Computer Science</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>From detection to grading: A hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF model for knee osteoarthritis diagnosis</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au0001"><name><surname>Perumalsamy</surname><given-names>Manikandaprabhu</given-names></name><email>manikandaprabhup@my.amrita.edu</email><xref rid="aff0001" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="cor0001" ref-type="corresp">&#x0204e;</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au0002"><name><surname>Govindarajan</surname><given-names>Priya</given-names></name><xref rid="aff0001" ref-type="aff">a</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au0003"><name><surname>Bran</surname><given-names>Rinhas</given-names></name><xref rid="aff0001" ref-type="aff">a</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au0004"><name><surname>KP</surname><given-names>Adarsh Krishna</given-names></name><xref rid="aff0001" ref-type="aff">a</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au0005"><name><surname>Jyothi</surname><given-names>Niranjan V</given-names></name><xref rid="aff0001" ref-type="aff">a</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au0006"><name><surname>Batumalay</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref rid="aff0002" ref-type="aff">b</xref></contrib><aff id="aff0001"><label>a</label>Department of Computer Science, School of Computing, Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, Mysuru, India</aff><aff id="aff0002"><label>b</label>Faculty of Data Science and IT, INTI International University, Nilai, Negeri Sembilan, Malaysia</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor0001"><label>&#x0204e;</label>Corresponding author. <email>manikandaprabhup@my.amrita.edu</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>19</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><!-- PMC Release delay is 0 months and 0 days and was based on <pub-date
						pub-type="epub">.--><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>12</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>19</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>15</volume><elocation-id>103725</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>13</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>16</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2025 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><copyright-holder/><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="abs0001"><p>This study presents a novel computer-aided diagnostic (CAD) system for detecting and grading the severity of knee osteoarthritis(KOA) from X-ray images, utilizing a hybrid deep learning and machine learning framework. The system combines YOLOv5 for precise knee joint localization and segmentation with a Random Forest classifier for ordinal Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) grading. Trained on a curated and augmented dataset of 1535 X-ray images, the model achieves an overall KL grading accuracy of 87 %. Evaluation includes ROC-AUC curves, Cohen&#x02019;s kappa scores, and grade-wise sensitivity and specificity metrics. This hybrid approach offers a scalable, interpretable, and clinically relevant tool for supporting radiologists in early KOA diagnosis, especially in resource-constrained settings.<list list-type="simple" id="celist0001"><list-item id="celistitem0001"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0001">Combines the powerful feature extraction capabilities of the YOLOv5 deep learning architecture with the classification strength of the Random Forest model.</p></list-item><list-item id="celistitem0002"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0002">YOLOv5 is used for knee joint segmentation to reduce background noise and improve classifier accuracy by focusing on the region of interest.</p></list-item><list-item id="celistitem0003"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0003">Achieves 87 % overall accuracy in KL grading, with enhanced sensitivity to subtle changes in early-stage KOA (Grades 1&#x02013;2).</p></list-item></list></p></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical" id="abs0002"><title>Graphical abstract</title><p><fig id="fig0011" position="anchor"><alt-text id="alttx001">Image, graphical abstract</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="ga1" id="celink0011"/></fig></p></abstract><kwd-group id="keys0001"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>Machine learning</kwd><kwd>Deep learning</kwd><kwd>YOLOv5</kwd><kwd>Random Forest</kwd><kwd>Image segmentation</kwd><kwd>Joint space narrowing</kwd><kwd>Early disease detection</kwd><kwd>Medical diagnosis</kwd><kwd>Computer-aided Diagnosis</kwd><kwd>Accessibility to Healthcare</kwd><kwd>Process innovation</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front><body><p id="para0005">Specifications table<table-wrap position="float" id="utbl0001"><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><tbody><tr><td valign="top"><bold>Subject area</bold></td><td valign="top">Computer Science</td></tr><tr><td valign="top"><bold>More specific subject area</bold></td><td valign="top">Machine Learning, Artificial Intelligence</td></tr><tr><td valign="top"><bold>Name of your method</bold></td><td valign="top">Hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF Model for Enhanced Detection and Classification of Knee Osteoarthritis Severity (Knee Osteoarthritis Severity using Hybrid Machine Learning Models on X-ray Images)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top"><bold>Name and reference of the original method</bold></td><td valign="top">Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) grading system for KOA classification [<xref rid="bib0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. YOLOv5 object detection algorithm by Ultralytics. Reference dataset: P. Chen, &#x0201c;Knee Osteoarthritis Severity Grading Dataset,&#x0201d; Mendeley Data, V1, 2018. doi: 10.17632/56rmx5bjcr.1 (These methods form the foundation for our hybrid diagnostic pipeline.)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top"><bold>Resource availability</bold></td><td valign="top"><list list-type="simple" id="celist0002"><list-item id="celistitem0004"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0006">Kaggle dataset: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/" id="interref0001">https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/</ext-link> (used in accordance with its license)-</p></list-item><list-item id="celistitem0005"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0007">YOLOv5 framework: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/ultralytics/yolov5" id="interref0002">https://github.com/ultralytics/yolov5</ext-link></p></list-item><list-item id="celistitem0006"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0008">Roboflow for annotation: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://roboflow.com" id="interref0003">https://roboflow.com</ext-link></p></list-item><list-item id="celistitem0007"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0009">Code environment: Visual Studio with Python, OpenCV, PyTorch</p></list-item><list-item id="celistitem0008"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="para0010">Model configuration files: yolov5s.pt, yolov5s.yaml, data.yaml</p></list-item></list></td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></p><sec id="sec0001"><title>Background</title><p id="para0011">Osteoarthritis (OA) is a complex, multifaceted disorder that presents significant challenges in diagnosis and treatment [<xref rid="bib0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>]. Knee osteoarthritis (KOA) is increasingly recognized as a major global public health concern, especially in India, due to its significant impact on disability and economic burden. By 2019, over 365 million people worldwide were affected by knee OA, resulting in a significant increase in Years Lived with Disability (YLDs) compared to previous decades. KOA poses particular challenges due to its widespread prevalence and its severe impact on mobility. Knee joint discomfort and swelling are common symptoms of KOA, and they are influenced by risk factors such as age, gender, heredity, obesity, and lifestyle [<xref rid="bib0002" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>]. According to recent studies, approximately 17 % of adults have KOA, with a higher incidence observed among women and those over the age of 60 [<xref rid="bib0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. Even with X-ray imaging, accurately diagnosing early-stage KOA is challenging due to subtle radiographic changes, such as minimal joint space narrowing and the initial development of osteophytes.</p><p id="para0012">The personal nature of manually interpreting these subtle signs often results in delayed diagnoses, hindering prompt intervention and effective disease management. A significant obstacle in KOA diagnosis is the challenge of identifying the earliest stages, which is often exacerbated by a weak correlation between pain symptoms reported and initial imaging results [<xref rid="bib0004" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>]. Although KOA diagnosis typically relies on patient-reported symptoms and imaging techniques such as X-rays [<xref rid="bib0005" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="bib0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>] and MRI [<xref rid="bib0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>,<xref rid="bib0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>], these methods often fail to detect early cartilage changes, highlighting the need for improved diagnostic tools. X-rays, by contrast, are more cost-effective and accessible than MRIs, offering faster processing and requiring less patient preparation. These properties make X-rays invaluable for early detection attempts, as illustrated in <xref rid="fig0001" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1</xref>.<fig id="fig0001"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>System architecture illustrating anatomical differences between normal and arthritic knee joints (femur, tibia, joint space).</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0001">Fig 1</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr1" id="celink0001"/></fig></p><p id="para0013">Advances in early and precise detection by automated technologies are crucial for reducing the substantial healthcare burden associated with late-stage KOA, enabling more effective resource allocation, and improving patient outcomes worldwide. The goal of this study is to create and validate a reliable computer-aided diagnosis method for knee osteoarthritis. This method combines the YOLOv5 and Random Forest models to analyze X-ray images, aiming to enhance the accuracy of KL grading, particularly for challenging early-stage classifications.</p><p id="para0014">Joint Space Narrowing (JSN), osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis are standard radiographic indicators used to diagnose knee osteoarthritis (KOA) [<xref rid="bib0008" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>]. Although these measurements have traditionally provided critical insights, their subjective interpretation and the subtle nature of early changes frequently result in inter-observer variability and delayed diagnosis. As a result, advances in imaging technology have accelerated the adoption of computer-aided diagnostic (CAD) technologies, increasing detection precision [<xref rid="bib0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. CAD systems incorporate advanced technologies, including image processing and computer vision, which encompass procedures such as image segmentation, joint localization, and feature extraction [<xref rid="bib0010" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>]. Early KOA classification methods employed conventional machine learning algorithms, such as Random Forest and Support Vector Machines (SVM), to extract texture and shape data from segmented images for categorization purposes [<xref rid="bib0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>].</p><p id="para0015">Deep learning techniques, utilizing pre-trained networks (e.g., ImageNet-based models), have significantly advanced feature extraction in KOA classification. Refining these networks on specific datasets enhances their focus on KOA classification [<xref rid="bib0012" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>]. However, early systematic approaches in orthopaedic arthritis were often constrained by the computational limitations of their time. Certain features, such as posterior and lateral osteophytes, were difficult to see or measure, necessitating complex and frequently unwieldy techniques. Although techniques such as histogram-based smoothing and specification [<xref rid="bib0013" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>] improved noise reduction, boundary detection, and ROI segmentation in bone images, their efficacy was frequently limited by the need for careful parameter adjustment and generalization.</p><p id="para0016">Similarly, early neural network-based approaches for arthritis detection [<xref rid="bib0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>], while promising, often suffered from limited validation across diverse datasets. An automated system utilizing MRI images for KOA identification and categorization, incorporating pre-processing techniques such as ROI cropping, cartilage region segmentation, and feature extraction like Grey Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM) texture, demonstrated impressive accuracies such as 98.5 % during training and 92 % during testing using an artificial neural network [<xref rid="bib0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>,<xref rid="bib0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]. Nonetheless, the expensive nature and restricted access to MRI scans limit their broad use, especially in settings with limited resources.</p><p id="para0017">Additional research categorized rheumatoid arthritis (RA) using frameworks that incorporated Support Vector Machines (SVMs) with radial basis function (RBF) kernels applied to X-ray images [<xref rid="bib0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>], classifying them into normal, abnormal, and severe RA classifications. While SVM-based techniques attained outstanding precision (100 %) for severe RA cases, their effectiveness in categorizing normal cases drastically declined, decreasing to &#x0003c;70 % [<xref rid="bib0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>]. This underscores a key limitation: existing methods struggle to distinguish subtle pathological changes from normal variations, emphasizing the need for classification algorithms with improved specificity and sensitivity across all severity levels. Alternative methods for arthritis identification, such as extracting Power Spectral Density (PSD) features and employing Independent Component Analysis (ICA), achieved approximately 70 % accuracy with logistic regression classifiers [<xref rid="bib0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. However, these methods still need to be improved to increase their specificity and sensitivity [<xref rid="bib0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>].</p><p id="para0018">Modern imaging techniques [<xref rid="bib0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>], such as MRI [<xref rid="bib0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>], CT, ultrasound, dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA), and positron emission tomography (PET), have undoubtedly enhanced diagnostic capabilities; however, their high cost and limited availability continue to be major barriers to widespread clinical adoption. Machine learning techniques have also been applied to the detection of rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and osteoarthritis (OA) [<xref rid="bib0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]. For example, studies utilizing MRI texture analysis to distinguish between RA and OA retrieved 279 features from the temporo-mandibular joint (TMJ) and reduced them to ten critical parameters. Despite promising results, these techniques were often limited by small sample sizes and subjective feature selection processes [<xref rid="bib0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]. Similarly, studies that used Random Forest models to classify synovial tissue samples from OA and RA X-ray images [<xref rid="bib0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>] by extracting histological features and cell density metrics [<xref rid="bib0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>] have shown promise. However, techniques based on single, isolated models frequently fail to provide the required robustness and generalization for real-world clinical applications, particularly across diverse patient demographics and image characteristics.</p><p id="para0019">This research work directly addresses and overcomes the aforementioned limitations by proposing a novel, robust computer-aided system for both grading the severity level of KOA and detecting OA with high accuracy. Existing automated methods and physician grading systems often misclassify Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) grades into adjacent categories due to subtle morphological differences [<xref rid="bib0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. Our approach directly addresses this challenge. This misclassification arises because successive KL grades exhibit subtle variations, complicating precise differentiation. Furthermore, mainstream studies often treat KL classification merely as a multi-class classification task, overlooking the inherent ordinal nature of these grades [<xref rid="bib0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]. Our approach explicitly models the ordinal structure of KL grades and combines YOLOv5 for precise knee joint localization with robust machine learning classifiers (Random Forest and SVM), enhancing diagnostic precision and clinical relevance.</p><p id="para0020">Despite the increasing incidence of knee osteoarthritis (KOA) globally, including in India, several significant research gaps remain. One critical gap is the absence of nationally representative data for India; most existing research has been confined to specific regions or population subgroups, leaving the actual prevalence and progression dynamics largely unknown. Furthermore, there is limited evidence supporting early diagnostic methods or affordable screening technologies suitable for low-resource environments. This gap contributes to delayed diagnosis and treatment, worsening patient outcomes and increasing healthcare burdens.</p></sec><sec id="sec0002"><title>Method details</title><p id="para0021">The proposed system comprises six key stages: dataset collection, preprocessing, bound boxing, joint localization via YOLOv5, feature extraction, and KL grading using Random Forest, as depicted in the Architectural Diagram shown in <xref rid="fig0002" ref-type="fig">Fig. 2</xref>.<fig id="fig0002"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>System architecture for the proposed system.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0002">Fig 2</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr2" id="celink0002"/></fig></p><p id="para0022">The dataset, sourced from Kaggle and expanded via Roboflow augmentation, includes 1535 labelled X-ray images spanning KL Grades 0&#x02013;4. All images were resized to 224 &#x000d7; 224 pixels and normalized using dataset-specific mean and standard deviation. YOLOv5 was trained to detect knee joints and generate bounding boxes, isolating regions of interest and reducing background noise. These segmented regions were then classified using a Random Forest model trained on extracted features. The KL grading task was treated as an ordinal classification problem, preserving the inherent progression of disease severity.</p></sec><sec id="sec0003"><title>Dataset collection</title><p id="para0023">The X-ray images used in this study were primarily obtained from the Kaggle database [<xref rid="bib0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. Initially, the dataset comprised approximately 700 high-quality knee X-ray images in PNG format. These images are automatically segmented and labelled using the Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) scale, which ranges from Grade 0 (Healthy) to Grade 4 (Severe), as illustrated in <xref rid="fig0003" ref-type="fig">Fig. 3</xref>. Low-quality or low-resolution photos were carefully eliminated from the label model to ensure accuracy and reliability. Image acquisition was crucial for establishing a set of images that accurately reflected the target population, enabling the model to accurately identify and categorize Knee Osteoarthritis (KOA) at various severity levels. For this research on "Detection and Classification of Knee Osteoarthritis using Random Forest and YOLOv5," the required X-ray images were primarily sourced from the Kaggle database [<xref rid="bib0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. Firstly, this dataset contained approximately 700 high-quality X-ray images of knees in PNG format. These images are automatically segmented and labelled according to the Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) scale, which ranges from Grade 0 (Healthy) to Grade 4 (Severe), shown in <xref rid="fig0003" ref-type="fig">Fig. 3</xref>.<fig id="fig0003"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>knee X-ray images for each KL grade (0&#x02013;4), illustrating osteophyte formation and joint space narrowing.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0003">Fig 3</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr3" id="celink0003"/></fig></p><p id="para0024">To label model accuracy and reliability, low-quality or low-resolution images were carefully removed. Image acquisition was crucial in creating a representative dataset that accurately reflected the target population, thereby enabling the model to effectively identify and classify Knee Osteoarthritis (KOA) at various severity levels. To improve the dataset's adaptability and diversity, and also to reduce overfitting, the dataset size was increased from 768 to 1535 photos utilizing Roboflow techniques, which are critical for efficiently training deep learning models.</p></sec><sec id="sec0004"><title>Data preprocessing</title><p id="para0025">Data preprocessing is a vital initial step in medical image analysis, as it prepares raw X-ray data for optimal machine learning efficacy. In this study, all images in the dataset were uniformly rescaled to 224 &#x000d7; 224 pixels to ensure consistency. This standardization enhances the consistency of the model's input dimensions, which is crucial for accurate and efficient data processing [<xref rid="bib0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]. Image pixel data, typically represented as integer values, was converted to floating-point numbers. This transformation is critical because machine learning models, particularly deep learning architectures, perform more accurate calculations using floating-point representations. Additionally, normalization was performed using the mean and standard deviation of the training dataset [<xref rid="bib0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. This procedure scales the input information to a consistent range, significantly improving a model's ability to learn hidden patterns and generate precise predictions by preventing the dominance of characteristics with broader ranges of numbers.</p></sec><sec id="sec0005"><title>Bounding box annotation</title><p id="para0026">To facilitate comprehensive model training, a broad array of knee X-ray images was obtained from various KOA patients and phases [<xref rid="bib0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>]. This diversity is vital, as it enables the model to learn from a wide range of challenges, thereby enhancing accuracy and generalizability in real-world situations. The Kellgren-Lawrence grading system was used to automatically categorize and classify the acquired images based on disease severity. Precise labelling is essential for training the model to differentiate between the various stages of KOA, as illustrated in <xref rid="fig0004" ref-type="fig">Fig. 4</xref>.<fig id="fig0004"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>Bounding box with predicted grade level.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0004">Fig 4</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr4" id="celink0004"/></fig></p><p id="para0027">Bounding box annotations were used to isolate and highlight the knee joint region of each X-ray picture [<xref rid="bib0033" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>]. This technique directs the model's attention solely to the important anatomical locations, thereby minimizing background noise and increasing classifier precision by emphasizing critical signs, such as joint space narrowing.</p></sec><sec id="sec0006"><title>Image segmentation</title><p id="para0028">Image segmentation is a crucial stage in diagnosing and assessing knee osteoarthritis (OA) [<xref rid="bib0030" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>], playing a vital role in accurately recognizing and evaluating the degradation of cartilage and other knee structures [<xref rid="bib0031" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>]. Numerous advanced segmentation techniques have been applied across imaging modalities, including MRI, to improve diagnostic accuracy and treatment planning for OA [<xref rid="bib0032" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>]. This work describes the YOLOv5 model's ability to accurately identify the knee joint and key anatomical landmarks within the bounding box, resulting in a refined region of interest for each analysis.</p></sec><sec id="sec0007"><title>YOLOv5 configuration for object detection</title><p id="para0029">Deep learning (DL), a specific subject of artificial intelligence (AI) [<xref rid="bib0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>], focuses on creating artificial neural networks that can learn hierarchical representations directly from enormous volumes of data. Deep learning models, compared to common machine learning algorithms, automatically extract complex patterns and information through numerous layers of nonlinear transformations [<xref rid="bib0029" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>]. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), a well-established deep learning architecture, are particularly useful for image classification and medical imaging tasks due to their inherent ability to recognize spatial and structural information. DL models are preferred due to their scalability, high precision, and ability to handle large volumes of data in high-dimensional datasets, making them extremely efficient for complex tasks such as disease detection [<xref rid="bib0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>] and image-based categorization [<xref rid="bib0034" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>].</p><p id="para0030">Object detection is a crucial feature of computer vision, as it extends beyond simple classification to determine not only what an object is, but also where it is located within an image. This added spatial complexity expands the task's scope and increases its computational demands. In the context of osteoarthritis detection, object detection is crucial for localizing and identifying early signs of the disease in medical images, such as X-rays, MRIs, and CT scans, thereby enabling a more focused analysis of the affected regions. Its versatile feature set and compatibility with libraries like PyTorch and OpenCV make YOLOv5 ideal for designing, debugging, and deploying object recognition models for early osteoarthritis detection.</p><p id="para0031">The YOLO (You Only Look Once) algorithm is well-known for its superior accuracy in real-time object detection. YOLOv5 was chosen for this work because of its excellent balance of speed and accuracy in detecting probable osteoarthritic alterations in X-ray images. YOLOv5 employs a one-shot detection technique, in which an advanced neural network predicts both bounding boxes and class labels in a single forward pass. This efficiency allows it to outperform traditional object detection models, which often require numerous passes.</p></sec><sec id="sec0008"><title>Model training</title><p id="para0032">The training is carried out using 'train.py', a script included with the YOLOv5 implementation. This script sets up the model, loads the dataset, and controls the entire training process, including saving the final model weights. The configuration file ('yolov5s.yaml') is critical, as it defines model architectural parameters, the number of classes (e.g., osteoarthritis stages denoted by bounding boxes), and image processing settings. The dataset consists of meticulously labelled images with bounding boxes indicating osteoarthritic regions that are then classified by severity for detection purposes. The 'train.py' script also performs necessary data preprocessing procedures, such as scaling and normalization, and displays real-time metrics, including loss, learning rate, and training time. It stores checkpoints at regular intervals and allows training to resume from the last saved state if interrupted.</p><p id="para0033">The final model weights are saved after training, allowing them to be used for inferring new medical images. To evaluate performance, the 'valid.py' script is used. It validates the trained model on a separate dataset by calculating key metrics, including accuracy, recall, and F1-score. These results shed light on the model's accuracy and reliability in detecting and classifying osteoarthritis. The 'valid.py' script is also useful for testing various model versions and fine-tuning hyperparameters to improve detection accuracy.</p></sec><sec id="sec0009"><title>A hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF model for knee osteoarthritis diagnosis</title><p id="para0034">Recent studies on the diagnosis of knee osteoarthritis (KOA) using artificial intelligence have primarily explored two methodological pathways: (i) image pre-processing and dataset augmentation to enhance input quality, and (ii) meticulous hyperparameter optimization to improve model performance. These strategies have been applied to both single-model deep learning systems and hybrid models that combine deep learning with conventional machine learning techniques.</p><p id="para0035">For instance, the study titled &#x0201c;Detection and Classification of Knee Osteoarthritis using X-Ray Images with Machine Learning Algorithms&#x0201d; employed a KOA-RF approach dedicated solely to classification [<xref rid="bib0037" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>]. While effective, such models often struggle with interpretability and sensitivity to noise, particularly in medical imaging tasks that involve subtle anatomical variations.</p><p id="para0036">To address these limitations, our proposed model introduces a hybrid two-stage system for KOA diagnosis. In the first stage, YOLOv5&#x02014;a state-of-the-art object detection framework&#x02014;is utilized to localize and segment the knee joint region in X-ray images. This step significantly reduces background noise, focusing the analysis on clinically relevant regions of interest. In the second stage, the segmented region is classified using a Random Forest (RF) model [<xref rid="bib0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>] based on the Kellgren&#x02013;Lawrence (KL) grading system.</p><p id="para0037">This hybrid approach leverages the powerful feature extraction capabilities of deep learning while retaining the interpretability and robustness of traditional machine learning. Compared to single CNN models, the YOLOv5-RF pipeline offers more explicit intermediate outputs (e.g., bounding boxes and segmentation maps), which can enhance transparency and support radiologist decision-making. While both architectures demonstrate high accuracy, the choice between them may depend on the availability of resources and the clinical context. In such cases, CNNs offer simplicity and speed, whereas the hybrid model provides greater diagnostic clarity and potential for expert validation.</p></sec><sec id="sec0010"><title>Methodological contributions</title><p id="para0038">Recent studies on KOA diagnosis using AI have primarily explored either single-model deep learning systems or hybrid approaches. For example, the study titled <italic>&#x0201c;Detection and Classification of Knee Osteoarthritis using</italic> X-Ray Images with Machine Learning Algorithms<italic>&#x0201d;</italic> [<xref rid="bib0037" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>] employed an end-to-end RF for KL grade classification. While effective, such models often struggle with interpretability and sensitivity to noise.</p><p id="para0039">In contrast, our proposed <italic>Hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF Model</italic> introduces a two-stage framework that explicitly separates detection and grading. YOLOv5 is used to localize the knee joint region, reducing background noise and enhancing feature extraction. The isolated region is then classified using a Random Forest model [<xref rid="bib0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>] based on the Kellgren&#x02013;Lawrence grading system [<xref rid="bib0026" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. This modular design improves transparency, supports explainability, and aligns more closely with radiological workflows. Its novelty lies in the decoupling of detection and grading, enabling more granular control and facilitating future integration of explainable AI techniques.</p><p id="para0040">To establish a solid benchmark for our proposed model, we examined the classification performance of a Joint Space Narrowing (JSN)-based learning model in comparison to traditional Feedforward Neural Networks (FNN), Artificial Neural Networks (ANN), and a Recurrent Neural Network (RNN). On the independent test dataset, a specific 'Hybrid model' achieved a test precision of 0.7811 and a weighted average F1 Score of 0.7833. These results suggest good performance, especially when compared to the baseline RNN, which produced much poorer results (test accuracy: 0.6391, weighted average F1-score: 0.6335) due to the non-sequential nature of the features.</p><p id="para0041">The 'Hybrid model' comparison performed similarly to the baseline FNN (test accuracy: 0.7870; weighted average F1-score: 0.7868). However, the comparative test demonstrated that the ANN model was the best architecture among these baselines for this specific dataset and feature set, with a top test accuracy of 0.8462 and a weighted average F1-score of 0.8451. A closer examination of the JSN-based model&#x02019;s classification report revealed stronger predictive performance for KL Grades 0&#x02013;2 than for Grades 3&#x02013;4, suggesting that while the model performs well overall, it may benefit from improved class separation at higher severity levels. <xref rid="fig0005" ref-type="fig">Fig. 5</xref> shows the mathematical formulations for these comparable neural network models.<fig id="fig0005"><label>Fig. 5</label><caption><p>Algorithm for proposed hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF model for KOA.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0005">Fig 5</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr5" id="celink0005"/></fig></p><p id="para0042">After successfully annotating various stages of bone degeneration and doubling the dataset size from 768 to 1536 images through Roboflow for augmentation, this enriched dataset was used for training and deployment. The dataset was split with a 70:30 training-to-validation ratio, and the YOLOv5 architecture was specifically utilized to train the object detection model. Two distinct approaches were considered and integrated for comprehensive implementation, focusing on enhancing the accuracy and robustness of osteoarthritis detection in bone imaging.</p><p id="para0043">The first approach integrates the Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) classification system, enabling classification across five severity levels (Grades 0&#x02013;4), as shown in <xref rid="fig0003" ref-type="fig">Fig.3</xref>. In parallel, bounding box techniques are used to reduce noise and improve classifier precision by isolating the joint gap and quantifying joint space narrowing with defined thresholds [<xref rid="bib0035" ref-type="bibr">35</xref>]. The study also comprehensively compares the performance of Support Vector Machine and Random Forest algorithms, highlighting their interpretability and robustness in handling complex medical images. The inherent characteristics of the Random Forest algorithm, when integrated with CNN-derived features, a 'Random Forest-based CNN approach', which aggregates multiple decision trees, proved to be a highly reliable choice for this application due to its ability to reduce overfitting and generalize well [<xref rid="bib0036" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>]. This integrated technique combines the powerful feature extraction capabilities of CNNs such as YOLOv5 with the robust classification characteristics of Random Forest, resulting in a powerful and effective system for KOA diagnosis.</p></sec><sec id="sec0011"><title>Method validation</title><p id="para0044">Knee osteoarthritis (KOA) remains a significant health concern, and the purpose of this study is to demonstrate the effectiveness and utility of detecting and classifying it using modern machine learning algorithms and image segmentation methods.</p><p id="para0045">Using a dataset of 1130 X-ray images, the proposed Hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF Model achieved significant accuracy and displayed substantial improvements over previous models assessed in this research. The training and testing phases began with an 80:20 data split, where 80 % of the dataset being used to train the Random Forest model. This stringent training process allowed the model to fine-tune its internal parameters, resulting in accurate classification. During training, the model learned to extract patterns and features from X-ray pictures that are useful in distinguishing distinct KOA stages. A well-prepared and meticulously labelled dataset considerably improved the model's ability to create the best and most accurate classifications, improving the reliability of KOA diagnosis with the Random Forest model.</p><p id="para0046">The proposed Hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF Model achieved an overall accuracy of 87 %, demonstrating its excellent interpretability and adaptability in handling complex medical imaging data. The methodology was successful in categorizing KOA into five separate Kellgren-Lawrence (KL) grading levels: Grade 0 (doubtful), Grade 1 (mild), Grade 2 (moderate), Grade 3 (moderately severe), and Grade 4. The model performs particularly well in classifying severity levels for Grades 0, 1, and 2. These categories are known to be extremely complex and difficult to differentiate due to subtle morphological differences, minimal joint space narrowing, and the inherent complexities of medical imaging. Finally, the pie chart in <xref rid="fig0006" ref-type="fig">Fig. 6</xref> illustrates the expected severity levels.<fig id="fig0006"><label>Fig. 6</label><caption><p>Confusion matrix of predicted severity level in the proposed random forest (KOA-RF) model.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0006">Fig 6</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr6" id="celink0006"/></fig></p></sec><sec id="sec0012"><title>Benchmarking and neural network comparisons</title><p id="para0047">To benchmark the performance of our proposed model, a comparative analysis was conducted against other classifiers, including a Stacked Dense and RNN Model, a Decision Tree, and YOLOv5 (for detection aspects). <xref rid="tbl0001" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref> summarizes the accuracy levels achieved by these various classifiers, including the proposed models. The YOLOv5 architecture, which is integrated into our system for precise knee joint detection and segmentation, significantly improves overall classification accuracy by providing refined regions of interest. While the Random Forest model focuses on KL grading, the YOLOv5 component achieved an accuracy of 96 % in detecting and localizing the knee joint, as indicated in <xref rid="tbl0001" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>. This high precision in bounding box prediction, as shown in <xref rid="fig0004" ref-type="fig">Fig. 4</xref>, significantly contributes to noise reduction, allowing subsequent classification efforts to focus on the most relevant anatomical structures, particularly aiding in the identification of subtle joint space narrowing.<table-wrap position="float" id="tbl0001"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Various classifier accuracy levels.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0007">Table 1</alt-text><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th valign="top">Level</th><th valign="top">Accuracy</th><th valign="top">Macro Average</th><th valign="top">Weighted Average</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td valign="top">Random Forest</td><td valign="top">87 %</td><td valign="top">90 %</td><td valign="top">87 %</td></tr><tr><td valign="top">Decision Tree</td><td valign="top">62 %</td><td valign="top">64 %</td><td valign="top">62 %</td></tr><tr><td valign="top">YOLOv5</td><td valign="top">96 %</td><td valign="top">94 %</td><td valign="top">90 %</td></tr><tr><td valign="top">Hybrid KOA-Yolov5-RF Model</td><td valign="top">97 %</td><td valign="top">95 %</td><td valign="top">91 %</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></p></sec><sec id="sec0013"><title>Performance of the hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF model</title><p id="para0048">The robustness of the Proposed Random Forest (KOA-RF) Model was further validated by testing it across different training-to-testing data split ratios: 60:40, 70:30, and 80:20, shown in <xref rid="fig0007" ref-type="fig">Fig.7</xref>(a), (b) &#x00026; (c), respectively. The results, illustrated in <xref rid="fig0008" ref-type="fig">Fig. 8</xref>, which compares test accuracy, consistently demonstrate that the algorithmic approach maintains stable performance across these varying data distributions. This consistency highlights the model&#x02019;s robust generalization capabilities and its reliability across different partitioning schemes.<fig id="fig0007"><label>Fig. 7</label><caption><p>(a) Confusion matrix of 60:40 approach, (b) Confusion matrix of 70:30 approach, (c) Confusion matrix of 80:20 approach.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0008">Fig 7</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr7" id="celink0007"/></fig><fig id="fig0008"><label>Fig. 8</label><caption><p>Model Test accuracy comparison.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0009">Fig 8</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr8" id="celink0008"/></fig></p><p id="para0049">To further assess the diagnostic performance of the proposed KOA-YOLOv5-RF model, we evaluated its classification capabilities using ROC-AUC and Cohen&#x02019;s kappa metrics. As illustrated in <xref rid="fig0009" ref-type="fig">Fig. 9</xref>, the ROC curves for each KL grade (0&#x02013;4) demonstrate strong discriminative ability, with AUC values exceeding 0.88 across all classes and peaking at 0.94 for Grade 0. This indicates the model&#x02019;s robustness in distinguishing between healthy and osteoarthritic cases, particularly in the early detection stage. Additionally, <xref rid="fig0010" ref-type="fig">Fig. 10</xref> presents the Cohen&#x02019;s kappa visualization, which yields a score of 0.82, reflecting substantial agreement between the model&#x02019;s predictions and the ground truth annotations. This metric highlights the system's reliability in replicating expert-level consistency in grading.<fig id="fig0009"><label>Fig. 9</label><caption><p>ROC curves for KL grades 0&#x02013;4.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0010">Fig 9</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr9" id="celink0009"/></fig><fig id="fig0010"><label>Fig. 10</label><caption><p>Cohen&#x02019;s kappa visualization. The proposed model achieved a kappa score of 0.76, indicating strong agreement with expert annotations.</p></caption><alt-text id="alt0011">Fig 10</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr10" id="celink0010"/></fig></p></sec><sec id="sec0014"><title>Performance stratification and radiologist comparison</title><p id="para0050">To provide a more granular understanding of model performance, we report sensitivity and specificity for each KL grade in <xref rid="fig0009" ref-type="fig">Fig.9</xref>. The model achieved 92 % sensitivity and 89 % specificity for Grade 0, 88 % and 85 % for Grade 1, 86 % and 84 % for Grade 2, 83 % and 81 % for Grade 3, and 90 % and 87 % for Grade 4. These results demonstrate strong discriminative ability across the KOA severity spectrum, with particular strength in early-stage detection. A stratified performance table is included to highlight grade-wise classification accuracy and support clinical interpretability. Additionally, a subset of 50 X-ray images was independently annotated by two radiologists. The model&#x02019;s predictions matched radiologist consensus in 84 % of cases, outperforming individual radiologist agreement (78 %), thereby underscoring its potential as a reliable diagnostic aid.</p><p id="para0051">The paper demonstrates that our proposed approach, which integrates a Random Forest-based CNN framework for classification, is highly capable of identifying and classifying knee arthritis. This condition affects approximately 15 % of the global population and around 17 % of young adults, primarily due to factors such as stress and irregular eating habits. Our system enables the early identification and potential treatment of KOA before it progresses to its most severe stage. Initial algorithmic tests yielded an accuracy of around 71 %, which was subsequently enhanced and improved to the desired 87 % accuracy for Random Forest. This significant improvement underscores the algorithmic robustness in managing high-complexity data, consisting of 1130 high-definition X-ray images of knees. The Random Forest algorithm's ability to effectively differentiate between various grades of arthritis has been made possible due to its inherent robustness and the advancements incorporated into our methodology.</p></sec><sec id="sec0015"><title>Clinical implications</title><p id="para0052">The proposed KOA-YOLOv5-RF system is designed to support radiologists in real-world diagnostic workflows. In a typical use case, the model can automatically flag early-stage KOA cases (Grades 1&#x02013;2) from routine X-ray screenings, prompting further expert review. This is particularly valuable in rural or resource-constrained settings where radiologist availability is limited. The system&#x02019;s bounding box outputs and KL grade predictions can be integrated into PACS environments, enabling seamless visualization and annotation. Prior studies have documented significant inter-observer variability in KL grading, especially in borderline cases. By providing consistent, interpretable predictions, our model can reduce diagnostic uncertainty and improve early intervention rates. Furthermore, the hybrid architecture offers transparency that aligns with clinical expectations for explainable AI.</p></sec><sec id="sec0016"><title>Limitations</title><p id="para0053">While the proposed hybrid KOA-YOLOv5-RF model demonstrates strong diagnostic performance, several limitations warrant attention. First, expanding the dataset&#x02019;s size and demographic diversity would improve generalizability and accessibility across populations. Second, integrating additional clinical biomarkers&#x02014;such as body mass index (BMI), age, and pain intensity&#x02014;could enhance predictive accuracy and clinical relevance. Third, incorporating finer-grained radiological features, including osteophyte formation and cartilage thinning, may improve sensitivity to early-stage KOA. Fourth, embedding explainable AI (XAI) techniques&#x02014;such as attention maps or saliency overlays&#x02014;can significantly enhance model interpretability and clinician trust. Finally, optimizing hardware compatibility and user interface design is essential for real-time deployment in resource-constrained clinical environments.</p></sec><sec id="sec0017"><title>Ethics statements</title><p id="para0054">This study used publicly available, anonymized knee X-ray datasets obtained from open-access repositories (e.g., Kaggle, Mendeley Data). No patient-identifiable information was accessed or processed at any stage of the process. As the data were fully anonymized and collected for research purposes, institutional ethical approval and informed consent were not required in accordance with local and international guidelines.</p></sec><sec id="sec10017"><title>CRediT authorship contribution statement</title><p id="para0054a"><bold>Manikandaprabhu Perumalsamy:</bold> Conceptualization, Methodology, Investigation, Supervision, Validation, Writing, Preparing the Original Draft, Reviewing, and Editing. <bold>Priya Govindarajan:</bold> Writing- Reviewing and Editing, Validity tests, Supervision. <bold>Rinhas Bran:</bold> Data labelling, Software, Validity tests. <bold>Adarsh Krishna KP:</bold> Data curation, Visualization, Validaity tests. <bold>Niranjan V Jyothi:</bold> Data collection, Validity tests, Software. <bold>M.Batumalay:</bold> Data curation, Reviewing and Editing.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="coi0001"><title>Declaration of competing interest</title><p id="para0063">The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</p></sec></body><back><ref-list id="cebibl1"><title>References</title><ref id="bib0001"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sbref0001"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shamir</surname><given-names>S.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ling</surname><given-names>W.W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Jr</surname><given-names>Scott</given-names></name><name><surname>W</surname><given-names>W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bos</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Orlov</surname><given-names>N.</given-names></name><name><surname>Macura</surname><given-names>T.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Eckley</surname><given-names>D.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferrucci</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Goldberg</surname><given-names>I.G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Knee x-ray image analysis method for automated detection of osteoarthritis</article-title><source>IEEE Trans. Biomed. Eng.</source><volume>56</volume><issue>2</issue><year>2009</year><fpage>407</fpage><lpage>415</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19342330</pub-id>
